course: french
lesson: 15
title: Pets and animals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Pets are short words in French: un chien is a dog and un chat is a cat, and since both start with ch, it is easy to say "Mon chat est un chien" by mistake.
explain: Every animal word has a gender that belongs to the word, not to the animal: une tortue is always une, even if your tortoise is a boy.
explain: Animal names can be funny pictures: a goldfish is un poisson rouge, "a red fish", and a guinea pig is un cochon d'Inde, "a pig from India".
explain: Picture a gate in Provence with the sign Attention au chien !: attention here means watch out, not pay attention, so you stay outside.
explain: Animals sound the same everywhere, but each language writes the noises differently: a French dog says ouaf ouaf and a French rooster says cocorico.
fact: un chien ("uhn SHYAN") is a dog and un chat ("uhn SHA") is a cat; a female dog is une chienne.
fact: You write le hamster, not l'hamster, because some words starting with h act as if they began with a consonant; others, like l'hôtel and l'homme, don't.
fact: un poisson rouge means a goldfish, word for word "a red fish".
fact: Attention au chien ! means "Beware of the dog!"; au is à + le.
fact: French animal noises: ouaf ouaf (woof woof), miaou (miaow), cocorico (cock-a-doodle-doo), meuh (moo), coin-coin (quack quack).
fact: Most words ending in -al change to -aux in the plural: un cheval, des chevaux (horses); un animal, des animaux.
fact: un lapin ("uhn la-PAN") is a rabbit; the end is a nasal sound and the n is not said; a girl rabbit is une lapine.
fact: une tortue is a tortoise or turtle and is always feminine, whether the animal is a boy or a girl.
fact: une souris ("un soo-REE") is a mouse; the s is silent; words ending in s, x or z don't change in the plural: des souris; souris is also a computer mouse.
fact: un oiseau ("uh-nwa-ZOH") is a bird and has all five vowels a, e, i, o, u; words ending in -eau add -x in the plural: des oiseaux.
fact: un cochon d'Inde is a guinea pig, word for word "a pig from India"; funnily, guinea pigs are not pigs at all, and they come from South America, not from India or from Guinea.
fact: Forest animals: le loup ("LOO") is the wolf, le renard ("ruh-NAR") the fox and l'ours ("LOORSS") the bear, where the s is said.
fact: Slang here: "dead weird" (North of England) means very strange; "sweet as" (New Zealand) means great; "y'all" (Texas) means you all; "sure" (Ireland) starts a sentence; "tidy" (Wales) means good; "yer" (Northern Ireland) means your; "howay, man" (Newcastle) means come on; "bare" (London) means lots of.
exercise: Ollie says "Mon chat est un chien." What did he say? | My cat is a dog | My dog is called Cat | My cat is lovely | My dog is a big dog
exercise: What is un chien? | a dog | a kitten | a chicken | a horse
exercise: How should Amira write "the hamster"? | le hamster | l'hamster | la hamster | les hamster
exercise: What does un poisson rouge mean? | a goldfish | a red pepper | a red poison | a red robin
exercise: Jake sees "Attention au chien !" on a gate. What does it mean? | Beware of the dog! | The dog is paying attention! | Please feed the dog! | The dog is sleeping!
exercise: How does a French dog bark in writing? | ouaf ouaf | woof woof | bow wow | wouf-wouf-wouf
exercise: What does a French rooster say? | cocorico | cock-a-doodle-doo | coin-coin | meuh
exercise: Noah draws "deux chevals". What is right? | deux chevaux | deux chevals | deux chevales | deux chevauxs
exercise: What is the plural of un animal? | des animaux | des animals | des animales | des animauxs
exercise: How does un lapin sound? | "uhn la-PAN", with a nasal end | "a LAP-in", with an English n said clearly at the end | "uhn la-PEEN" | "un LAP-ane"
exercise: Tyler says "J'ai un tortue." What is right? | J'ai une tortue. | J'ai un tortue. | J'ai une tortu. | J'ai un tortues.
exercise: Why is it une tortue even for a boy tortoise? | The word tortue is feminine, whatever the animal | All animals are feminine in French | Boy tortoises are rare in France | It isn't: a boy tortoise is un tortue
exercise: How do you say "a mouse"? | une souris, said "soo-REE" | un souris, said "soo-REESS" | une souri, said "SOO-ree" | une sourice, said "soo-REESS"
exercise: What is the plural of une souris? | des souris | des sourises | des sourix | des souriss
exercise: What is un oiseau? | a bird | a little fish | a goose | a sheep
exercise: How do you say un oiseau? | "uh-nwa-ZOH" | "uhn oy-see-OW" | "uhn wee-SOH" | "uhn OY-zoo"
exercise: What is un cochon d'Inde? | a guinea pig | a pig from Paris | a wild boar | an Indian elephant
exercise: What is le loup? | the wolf | the fox | the bear | the hairdo
test: The class falls about when Ollie says "Mon chat est un chien." Which tip helps? | Say them in pairs with pictures: chien, dog; chat, cat | Always say chat when you mean any pet | Never talk about pets in French | Use the English words, they're easier
test: Amira says writing le hamster is "dead weird". ("Dead" means very.) What is going on? | A few h words act as if they began with a consonant | hamster is a feminine word | The h is said with a big puff | hamster is an English word, so it takes le
test: Ella says, "Sweet as, but it's gold!" ("Sweet as" means great.) What is the French for her goldfish? | un poisson rouge | un poisson or | un poisson doré rouge | un or poisson
test: Jake asks, "Y'all, is the dog paying attention?" ("Y'all" means you all.) What could happen if he walks in? | A guard dog might rush out at him | The dog will do tricks for him | Nothing, the sign is about a dog show | The dog will pay him attention points
test: Lucy says, "French dogs say ouaf ouaf? Sure ours say woof." What's the truth? | Dogs sound the same; the languages write it differently | French dogs really make a different noise | Only French puppies say ouaf | The French are hearing a different animal
test: Noah says, "Ow, tidy, but why chevaux?" ("Tidy" means good.) What's the rule? | Most -al words change to -aux in the plural | Every animal word adds -x in the plural, like oiseaux | Horses are special in all languages | Long words change their ending to -oux
test: Ruby jokes lapin is "a lap-in", because "it does sit on yer lap". How should she say it? | "la-PAN", with a nasal end and no n | "LAP-in", like the English joke | "la-PEEN", like a pin | "lap-ANNE", with a clear n
test: Tyler asks, "Wait, my turtle's a girl now?" What's the truth? | No, the word tortue is feminine, whatever the animal is | Yes, all turtles in France are girls, so the word is too | Yes, he must call his turtle a girl's name | No, tortue means a boy turtle
test: Sam's mate goes, "Howay, man," when Sam reads souris as "soo-riss, like a mouse in a sauce". ("Howay, man" means come on.) What's wrong? | The final s is silent: "soo-REE" | The ou says "ow": "sow-REE" | The word is said "SAW-ris" | Nothing, he said it right, the s is always said
test: Olivia says oiseau has "bare vowels". ("Bare" means lots of.) How many different vowels does it have? | 5: a, e, i, o, u | 3: o, i, e | 4: o, i, e, a | 6: o, i, s, e, a, u
test: Leo says, "An Indian pig? That's not a guinea pig, mate!" What's the funny truth? | English calls them guinea pigs though they aren't pigs or from Guinea | The French call them Indian pigs because they really are pigs | Guinea pigs come from India, so the French are right | The French word means "pig of the night"
test: ★★★ Grace makes a poster of animals in the plural. Which list has NO mistake? | des chevaux, des oiseaux, des souris | des chevals, des oiseaux, des souris | des chevaux, des oiseaus, des souris | des chevaux, des oiseaux, des sourises
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the grammatical gender of animal nouns in French? | It belongs to the noun and is independent of the sex of the animal | It is determined in each instance by the sex of the animal | It is masculine for all animals without exception | It is feminine for all domestic animals
exam: Identify the plural form of the noun cheval. | chevaux | chevals | chevales | chevalx
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the inscription Attention au chien. | It constitutes a warning of the presence of a dog | It constitutes an invitation to train a dog | It constitutes a notice that a dog is for sale | It constitutes a request that the dog be fed
exam: It is given that a noun terminates in the letter s, as in souris. Determine its plural form. | des souris, the noun being unchanged | des sourises, with the addition of -es | des sourix, with the addition of -x | des sourisses, with the doubling of s
